  "body": "## What happens\n\nThe probing macro in the Mazak post jumps past each alarm with `IF[...]GOTO<n>`. `alarmUnless` writes the jump target with `block([\"N\" + probeLabel])`. When the option \"Sequence numbers\" is \"Every block\", `block()` puts the next sequence number in front of each line. The target line then is `N250 N9001`. The control reads the first N word, so `GOTO9001` does not find its target and the program stops.\n\nAn every-block number can also be the same as a probe label (for example a start of 1 and a step of 1000).\n\n## Expected\n\n- The label line has only the label word.\n- The every-block numbers never use a number that is a probe label.\n\n## Evidence (upstream main `eafe6aec6`)\n\n- `assets/posts/mazak.cps:1006-1017`: `probeLabel` and `alarmUnless`.\n- `assets/posts/mazak.cps:187-210`: `block()` adds the sequence-number prefix to each line that is not a comment.\n- `assets/posts/mazak.cps:168-172`: `nextSeq()`.\n\nWorkaround: set \"Sequence numbers\" to a value other than \"Every block\" for a probing program.\n\nFound by the post audit against the Mazak manuals (#4130).\n\n## Done means\n\nA probing golden with `showSequenceNumbers: every-block` shows each `GOTO<n>` target as a line that starts with `N<n>`, and shows no N number two times.\n",
